/*
 * The declarations the compiler references - direction 2 of `role.h`.
 *
 * A complete declaration, written in the library, that the compiler has to be able to *name*: the
 * classes the language's own syntax dispatches through, the functions it inserts calls to, the
 * records and constructors it builds. Nothing here is implemented by the compiler; what it needs is
 * a handle, and the handle is what a rename used to take away silently.
 *
 * The row is claimed by `@builtin(<enumerator>)` on the declaration, and the enumerator's own name
 * is the spelling - see `YANA_ROLE_TEXT`. There is no name column, so there is nothing to drift.
 *
 * ## The columns
 *
 *  - `role` is the enumerator, the attribute's spelling, and the order of the enum.
 *  - `entity` is what kind of declaration may claim it - checked where the attribute is read.
 *  - `arity` is how many parameters the head has, or how many arguments a call to it writes. Zero
 *    where the role does not constrain it.
 *  - `members` is how many members a class must declare, or how many constructors a record must -
 *    the same dependency read at whichever kind of declaration the row is about, since both are
 *    reached by position. Zero where the count is the library's business.
 *  - `payload` is how many fields each of a record's constructors carries, where `members` states
 *    a count. The compiler reads a payload by position exactly as it reads the constructor, so a
 *    row that numbers the constructors has to say what each holds; zero for a class.
 *  - `teardown` says the role's member runs because a value died. It decides three things the
 *    compiler would otherwise work out by comparing against a list of enumerators in four places: a
 *    member of it is a teardown, its subject is consumed rather than borrowed, and it may not be
 *    called by name from source. False for every role that is not an ownership class.
 *  - `presence` is what absence means - `role.h`'s `Presence`.
 *
 * `arity` and `members` are the whole of the shape check for a `Known` role, and deliberately so.
 * The declaration is the library's to write; what the compiler depends on is that it can index the
 * head and reach the member it calls, and those two numbers are exactly that dependency. An
 * `Op` role, where the compiler supplies the body, has a real signature to check and `op.def` is
 * where that lives.
 *
 * A record stating a `members` count carries one thing more, and `checkRoles` is where it is said
 * rather than here: a role whose constructors the compiler *numbers* needs them numbered from zero,
 * since what it holds is an index and not a handle.
 *
 * ## Members are reached by position
 *
 * Every class row below states a member count, and the compiler reaches a member by its index in
 * the declaration - `Num`'s `+` is member zero, `Ord`'s `compare` is member four. That is one
 * dependency on the library's *order* rather than a name per member, and it is the same one the
 * ownership classes already had at one member each: the alternative is a spelling of every member
 * in this directory, which is the table of library names the directory exists to not be. The
 * order is the real `lib/Core`'s, and `prelude/` copies each class head and member list verbatim.
 */

/*
 * The three the ownership classifier reaches for - `doc/spec/ownership.md`.
 *
 * `Reclaim` releases a value's own storage and may be elided where something reclaims it in bulk;
 * `Drop` runs an effect at last use and is never elided; `Copy` is not a teardown at all and is here
 * because it *subtracts* the same capability the other two do - an authored `Copy` subtracts
 * `TrivialCopy`, so the classifier has to see it on the same walk.
 *
 * `Required`, because the classifier asks these three about every type in every program: a stage
 * whose library does not declare them cannot compile anything, so saying so once at the end of the
 * declaration passes is the whole of what a program with no `Drop` needs to hear. They were
 * *synthesized* in C++ until `prelude/` existed - a second implementation of a class declaration,
 * belonging to no module so that it could be found from every one - and the prelude declares them
 * instead, which is what makes a declared one and a stood-in one the same class by construction
 * rather than by agreement.
 *
 * One member each: `drop(->value: a)`, `reclaim(->value: a)`, `copy(from: a) -> a`.
 */
//              role     entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( reclaim, Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      true,    Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( drop,    Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      true,    Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( copy,    Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * And the two the classifier *answers* rather than reads - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s two implicit
 * ownership classes.
 *
 * Nobody writes an instance of either and nobody may: they hold for a type exactly when they hold
 * for every one of its members, which is the walk `Reclaim`, `Drop` and `Copy` above feed. They are
 * classes so that a *signature* can constrain a variable by one - `fn (TrivialCopy(a)) dup(x: a)`
 * - and Design-Memory §2.1 is what makes that load-bearing rather than decorative: an unconstrained
 * parameter is treated as non-TrivialCopy inside its own body whatever a caller substitutes, so a
 * declared constraint is the only thing that can say otherwise.
 *
 * Zero members, exactly like the atomics' pair further down, and answered the same way -
 * `structuralAnswer`. The shipping stage reaches them through `Program::coreClasses` by name, which
 * is what these two rows replace for the stage that reads the attribute instead.
 *
 * **`Optional`**, for the reason the atomics' rows are: `prelude/` declares neither, and a stage
 * pointed at it is a stage whose programs may not constrain on them - which is a smaller library
 * rather than a missing one. Nothing the compiler itself does needs the *class*; the classification
 * is `Ownership`'s and exists whether or not a declaration names it.
 */
//              role         entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( trivialCopy, Entity::Class, 1,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( trivialSink, Entity::Class, 1,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )

/*
 * What an unadorned literal is - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `class FromInt(a = Int)` and
 * `class FromDecimal(a = Float)`.
 *
 * The compiler needs these for their **head defaults** and for nothing else yet. `1` has no width of
 * its own and `1.5` has no precision of its own, so what one *is* where no position asks for
 * anything is a statement the language makes, and `doc/spec/expressions.md` makes it by pointing at
 * these two declarations rather than by naming a type. A parameter default is not decoration here:
 * it is the statement.
 *
 * Written out in the compiler until this row existed, as `intType(32, true)` and `floatType(64)`,
 * and **the second was wrong**: Core's `Float` is `F32`, so `let x = 1.5` was an `F64` in this stage
 * and an `F32` in the one that reads the real library - the same source, two programs, and nothing
 * to say so. It is the fixity divergence again, one tier down, and it was found the same way.
 *
 * `Required` for the reason the ownership classes are: a stage whose library declares neither cannot
 * give a bare `1` a type at all. One member each, which is the conversion itself - the compiler does
 * not call either yet, and the day it does is the day a literal at a *declared* type dispatches
 * through them instead of being a constant of it.
 */
//              role         entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( fromInt,     Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( fromDecimal, Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The truth type - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `data Bool = False | True`.
 *
 * An **ordinary declared enum**, and the whole of what this row buys is that the compiler can name
 * it. `Bool` used to be a row in `primitive.def`, on the argument that "a comparison has to answer
 * something and this stage may have no declarations to answer with" - which stopped being true the
 * day `prelude/` existed, and cost a type kind, a constant kind and its two constructors' spellings
 * to keep saying. A comparison answers whatever declaration claimed this, `if` reads one, and the
 * manifestation layer gives a two-constructor enum one byte the way it gives every other one.
 *
 * **Two constructors, false first.** That is what `members` states, and `checkRoles` is where the
 * rest of it is checked: the compiler emits comparisons whose result is 0 or 1, so the constructor
 * numbered zero is the false one and the constructor numbered one is the true one. Which *words*
 * those are is the library's - nothing here spells `True`, and a dump prints whatever the
 * declaration called them.
 */
//              role     entity          arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( boolean, Entity::Record, 0,    2,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The growable array - `lib/Core/Array.native.yana`'s `data Array(a)`, and `[a]`'s other spelling.
 *
 * A **record** role rather than a class one, and the first of them. What the compiler needs is a
 * handle: `[a]` is a type the language's own grammar spells, so the bracket form has to resolve to
 * something, and `doc/spec/collections.md` is explicit that "`[a]` and `Array(a)` are one type".
 *
 * `Required` and declared, like every other row. `prelude/Core/Core.yana` writes `data Array(a)`
 * with this attribute on it and `lib/Core/Array.native.yana` writes the same attribute on the real
 * one, so the two are the same declaration to everything above - which is the property the bracket
 * spelling needs: `doc/spec/collections.md` is explicit that "`[a]` and `Array(a)` are one type",
 * and a compiler that invented a second one would have made them two.
 *
 * What the compiler decides about it is the **window** - `AccessForm::View`, whose two
 * words are a manifestation and not a type - and nothing else. What an *owner* holds is the
 * library's business and differs by target, which is why the fields are not this table's to state.
 */
//              role     entity          arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( array,   Entity::Record, 1,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * What a shared or unique `[a]` **is** - the array's view record, `prelude/Core/Array.yana`'s
 * `data ArrayView(a)`, and the row above's other half.
 *
 * `array` names the *owner*. This names the **representation record** a window manifests as: an
 * occurrence of `Array(a)` under `AccessForm::View` is laid out as this record, its fields are what
 * `xs.length` on a window reaches, and a value of this record converts to that occurrence and to
 * nothing else. That is `doc/proposal/Analysis-Manifestations.md` Part 8's arrangement, and it is
 * what keeps this from being a second type for `Array(a)`: nothing resolves `[a]` to it, a window
 * is still `Array(a)` under a contract, and the declaration has to be `@local` so that the name
 * cannot be written outside the module that declares the owner.
 *
 * **The compiler reads no field of it.** What a window's extent is, where its elements are and how
 * one is narrowed are library bodies - `length`, `Index.get`, `slice` - so the columns here pin
 * nothing about the field list: `members` is zero because the count is the library's business,
 * and `checkDeclaredRoles` checks what a view record has to be instead, which is `@local`, one
 * constructor, no field that owes a teardown and no instance. `resolve_mir/type.cpp`'s view table
 * is where this row is paired with the owner it manifests and the class members that derive it.
 *
 * `Required`, because `[a]` is grammar: a library that declares no window is one in which no
 * container can be passed, and saying so once is better than a failure at the first parameter.
 */
//              role       entity          arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( arrayView, Entity::Record, 1,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The string, and what a shared one is - `prelude/Core/String.yana`'s `data String` and its
 * `@local data StringView`.
 *
 * `String` is a declaration on `Array`'s terms, not a kind of the compiler's: a record claiming
 * this row, whose fields are the library's, and the only two things the compiler knows about it
 * are that a literal is one and that its shared occurrence manifests as the view record beside it.
 * A `String` in a binding position that is shared is the **view** - there is no bracket spelling to
 * ask for one, so a type with a shared view row defaults to it - and `&s: String` or an owned one
 * is the owner, whose fields `push` reaches. That default is the one place a view row and a window
 * row differ, and `resolve_mir/type.cpp`'s table says which is which.
 *
 * `Required`, because a string literal is grammar and has to be a value of something.
 */
//              role        entity          arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( string,     Entity::Record, 0,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( stringView, Entity::Record, 0,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * How an owner becomes its view - the derivations, which are class members the compiler calls at
 * the one adaptation site it has.
 *
 * `Contiguous(c -> a)`'s member zero is `elements(self: c) -> '[a]`, and `Writable(c -> a)`'s is
 * `elementsMut(&self: c) -> &[a]`: an owner reaching a shared or unique `[a]` position is a call to
 * one of those, exactly as `lib/Core/Container.yana` already declares them. `Viewed(a)`'s member
 * zero is `view(self: a) -> 'a`, the same statement for a type whose view has no spelling of its
 * own, which today is `String`. **The subject of that member is the owner** - a shared `String` is
 * the view everywhere else, and the body that builds the view out of the owner's fields is the one
 * body that cannot take one - and `resolveOccurrence` reads this role to say so.
 *
 * `Index(c -> k, v)` is `xs[i]` on anything that is not a fixed array: member zero is `get` and
 * member one is `getMut`, and which one a subscript reaches is decided by what the position asks
 * for. A fixed array's element stays a place of the compiler's; a window's is the library's, which
 * is where its bounds check is.
 *
 * All four `Required`, because each is reached by grammar rather than by name.
 */
//              role        entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( contiguous, Entity::Class, 2,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( writable,   Entity::Class, 2,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( viewed,     Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( index,      Entity::Class, 3,    2,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The functions the compiler inserts calls to, for the two pieces of grammar that are calls.
 *
 * `stringLiteral(bytes: %CodeUnit, length: Size) -> String` is what `"abc"` is: the bytes are a
 * constant the compiler places, and the value is whatever the library builds out of an address and
 * a count. `slice(self: '[a], from: Size, to: Size) -> '[a]` and its unique twin are `xs[a..b]`,
 * where the clamping and the arithmetic are the body's. The compiler holds the arity and nothing
 * else; the first is `arity` two and the other two are three.
 */
//              role          entity            arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( stringLiteral, Entity::Function, 2,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( slice,         Entity::Function, 3,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( sliceMut,      Entity::Function, 3,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * Where a check that did not hold stops the program - `prelude/Core/Array.yana`'s `checkFailed`.
 *
 * The one role here the compiler never *calls*. The shipping stage emits `checkCondition(index >=
 * length)` at every subscript and holds a pointer to that function so it can find its own calls
 * again; the ownership stage inserts nothing at all, because `Index.get` writes the test itself and
 * `@inline(always)` grafts it into the caller. What arrives is an ordinary comparison, an ordinary
 * branch, and an arm that calls this - so what the role is for is *recognition* rather than
 * emission: `-no-checks` has to be able to say which branch is a check, and comparing a callee
 * against a declaration is the only statement of that which a rename cannot silently unfill.
 * `resolve_ssa/opt/check.cpp` is the one reader, and its file comment is the structural rule.
 *
 * `arity` zero because the declaration takes nothing: a check that fails says its exit status and
 * no more, which is `lib/Core/Check.yana`'s argument - formatting a message would put the format
 * machinery in the binary of a program that never prints, and would recurse through the bounds
 * check inside the formatter.
 *
 * `Optional`, on the allocator rows' terms and for a stronger reason than theirs: a library that
 * declares no `checkFailed` is a library whose containers do not stop the program, which is a
 * smaller library rather than a broken one. What an unfilled row costs is that `-no-checks` finds
 * nothing to discharge, which is exactly what it should find.
 */
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( checkFailed,   Entity::Function, 0,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )

/*
 * The heap, as three functions - Design-Allocator.md §18.5's binding, and the roles that replace
 * `findNative("allocateHeap", 12)`.
 *
 * `allocateHeap(size: Size) -> %U8` and `freeHeap(allocation: %U8)` are what
 * `StorageClass::Heap` means: an allocation the compiler emits because the program's *shape* needs
 * one - a `@box` field, a container's run - rather than because the source wrote a call.
 * §18.2 is the argument for a role over a name, and it is the one this replaces: a name lookup
 * cannot say what shape it wanted, so a wrongly declared `freeHeap` used to be reported as an
 * ordinary argument-count failure inside an unrelated user function, and a missing one turned the
 * heap off with the corpus green.
 *
 * ## `isHeapAddress`, and the two compilation modes
 *
 * `isHeapAddress(address: %U8) -> Bool` is the **optional** third, and what it decides is how a
 * boxed field's storage is released when the release site cannot see where the value was placed -
 * an overwrite through a `&` borrow, which is handed bytes and a type and nothing about the
 * caller's frame.
 *
 * A provider that answers it is a provider whose allocations are recognizable from the address
 * alone - a reserved, aligned virtual window, or a page directory beside it. The compiler then
 * stores **no** provenance at all: the release is `if isHeapAddress(p) then freeHeap(p)`, and a
 * record with a boxed field is exactly its fields.
 *
 * A provider that does not answer it gets the other mode: every record with a boxed field carries
 * one hidden bit per box saying whether that allocation is the program's to hand back, written
 * where the box was made and read where it dies. Correct everywhere and one bit wider.
 *
 * **Which mode a build is in is decided by whether this role is filled**, which is known before any
 * body is lowered - the provider is selected by `@allocator` and the file axis long before that. So
 * neither mode is a runtime test, and a program never contains both.
 *
 * All three are `Optional` rather than `Required`, and for the reason the tail of this file gives:
 * a build reading a library that declares no heap has no heap, and a row that insisted otherwise
 * would be a `Presence` that lies. What a program that needs one and has none gets is a diagnostic
 * at the allocation, naming the missing role.
 */
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( allocateHeap,  Entity::Function, 1,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( freeHeap,      Entity::Function, 1,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( isHeapAddress, Entity::Function, 1,   0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )

/*
 * The classes an operator dispatches through - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `Eq`, `Ord` and `Num`, and
 * `lib/Core/Bits.yana`'s `Bitwise`, `Integral` and `Truth`.
 *
 * `doc/spec/expressions.md`: "Operators are resolved by the ordinary overload rules: an operator
 * name is a member of an overload set keyed by `(name, arity)`". So `a + b` finds `+` exactly as
 * `size(x)` finds `size` - by name, through the imports - and the compiler needs none of these
 * rows to *read* an operator. What it needs them for is the other direction, `provided.def`: the
 * instances at the primitive types are the compiler's to generate, and generating `Num(I32)` means
 * being able to name `Num` and reach its `+` by position.
 *
 * Three of the compiler's own constructs reach members the same way, with no operator written:
 * a **condition** is `Truth.truthy` of the tested value; a **literal pattern** compares with
 * `Eq.==` and a range pattern with `Ord`'s four; and a **counted `for`** tests its bound with
 * `Ord` and advances with `Num`. Each is one member index below, which is the whole of what
 * `doc/spec/expressions.md`'s "a condition is a call to `Truth`" and `doc/spec/patterns.md`'s
 * "equality is the type's own `Eq` instance" ask of the compiler.
 *
 * The member order is Core's, and the emitters index it:
 *
 *  - `Eq`: `==`, `!=`.
 *  - `Ord`: `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `compare`, `min`, `max`.
 *  - `Num`: `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, unary `-`.
 *  - `Bitwise`: `and`, `or`, `xor`, `not`.
 *  - `Integral`: `%`, `shl`, `shr`, `sar`, `rol`, `ror`.
 *  - `Truth`: `truthy`.
 *
 * `Required`, because a library without them is a library whose `+` means nothing, and a program
 * compiled against one would be told "unknown function +" at every arithmetic expression rather
 * than what is actually wrong. The report names the role and no source position, as for `drop`.
 */
//              role      entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( eq,       Entity::Class, 1,    2,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( ord,      Entity::Class, 1,    7,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( num,      Entity::Class, 1,    5,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( bitwise,  Entity::Class, 1,    4,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( integral, Entity::Class, 1,    6,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( truth,    Entity::Class, 1,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * What `compare` answers - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `data Ordering = LT | EQ | GT`.
 *
 * Here for one reason: the compiler generates `Ord`'s instances at the primitive types, and the
 * body it generates for `compare` has to *construct* one of these. Which words the three are is the
 * library's; what the row states is that there are three, numbered from zero in the order a
 * comparison answers them - less, equal, greater - and carrying nothing, which is what lets a
 * generated body write the constructor's index and a dump print its name.
 */
//              role      entity          arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( ordering, Entity::Record, 0,    3,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The three conversion classes - `lib/Core/Convert.yana`'s `Widen`, `Narrow` and `Bitcast`.
 *
 * `::` is one operator over three classes, and which class relates a pair of types is the whole of
 * the rule for what it does: `Widen` is the lossless direction and the only one that also happens
 * without being written, `Narrow` is the lossy one and must be written, and `Bitcast` is neither -
 * the same bits read as another type. `doc/spec/core.md`'s Conversion section.
 *
 * Here for `provided.def`'s sake, exactly as `Num` and `Eq` are: the instances at the primitive
 * types are the compiler's to generate, because `I32`'s widening cannot be written in terms of
 * anything more basic, and generating one means being able to name the class and reach its member.
 * Which *pairs* get which class is not this table's - it is `intrinsic/convert.h`, where the rule is
 * stated once for both resolve stages.
 *
 * **Arity two and no functional dependency**, which is the shape that makes the result type
 * selecting: nothing about `widen(x)`'s argument says which rung was meant, so the position it
 * stands in decides, and a class keyed on both parameters is what lets it. One member each -
 * `widen`, `truncate`, `bitcast` - and the method of the lossy one is named for what happens to the
 * *value* rather than for what happens to the type, which is the part a reader needs.
 *
 * `Required`: a language whose `::` cannot widen has no conversions at all, and an implicit widening
 * is asked for at every call boundary in every program.
 */
//              role     entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( widen,   Entity::Class, 2,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( narrow,  Entity::Class, 2,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( bitcast, Entity::Class, 2,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * What a skipping traversal answers with - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `data Outcome(a, e) =
 * Proceed(a) | Exit(e)`.
 *
 * A `lens fn f(...) -> (T | E)` hands its continuation's answer back inside this, and the compiler
 * is what builds the value and what reads it: `Proceed` carries the exit signal and `Exit` carries
 * what the traversal failed with. It used to be *synthesized* - a record per skipping lens, built
 * in `resolve_mir/resolve_sig.cpp` with the two constructor names spelled there - on the argument
 * that nothing outside that function constructed or matched it. That was true and is still not a
 * reason for the compiler to hold a second declaration of a type the library already declares:
 * `declareOutcome` instantiates this one at `(exit signal, E)` now, and a program that names
 * `Proceed` in a pattern against a lens's result is naming the constructor the library declared.
 *
 * Two constructors, one field each, numbered from zero with `Proceed` first: the SSA conversion
 * reads the discriminant by index, and "proceeding is the first case" is the order every `Outcome`
 * in the analysis documents is written in.
 */
//              role      entity          arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( outcome,  Entity::Record, 2,    2,      1,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The two classes `?` and `?.` dispatch through - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `Try` and `Rewrap`.
 *
 * `Try(m -> a, e)` relates a carrier to the two things it can be: `toOutcome` says which path a
 * value is on and `fromExit` rebuilds the carrier from a reason. Two members, in that order, and the
 * order is the dependency - `resolve_mir/resolve_try.cpp` reads them by index, which is the same
 * arrangement every other class role here is under.
 *
 * `Rewrap(m, b -> n)` is the direction `Try` cannot state and is what makes `?.` a different
 * operator rather than a spelling of the first: a chain containing one comes back *wrapped*, so
 * something has to say which type `m`'s wrapper around `b` is. One member.
 *
 * Both are three parameters keyed on their deciding prefix rather than a type constructor applied to
 * a variable, which is the higher-kinded machinery this language does not have. That is what makes
 * `Maybe(Row)` plus `String` giving `Maybe(String)` an ordinary head match.
 *
 * **`carrier` and not `try`**, which is the one row here whose spelling is not its class's name.
 * `try` is a C++ keyword, so it cannot be an enumerator, and a spelling column would be exactly the
 * second name for one thing that this file exists to not have. What the role is named for instead is
 * what the class *says* - which of a carrier's two paths a value is on - and `doc/spec/core.md`
 * calls the subject a carrier in the same words.
 *
 * `Required`, because the operators are grammar: a program that writes `?` in a language whose Core
 * declares no `Try` has no diagnostic worth reading, and a missing row would be reported as an
 * unknown name at the one place that cannot say what is actually wrong.
 */
//              role      entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( carrier,  Entity::Class, 3,    2,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( rewrap,   Entity::Class, 3,    1,      0,      false,   Presence::Required )

/*
 * The two closed classifications of `Atomic` - `lib/Atomic/Atomic.native.yana`, Analysis-Atomics.md
 * §3.2.
 *
 * `AtomicValue(a)` is the family an atomic may hold - the integers, a payload-free enumeration, a
 * raw pointer - and `AtomicInteger(a)` is the arithmetic subset of it. They are **structural**, on
 * the terms `TrivialCopy` is: no instance of either is ever written, the compiler answers them from
 * the shape of the type, and an instance saying otherwise would be a claim it has already
 * contradicted. Class spelling all the same, so that a generic signature can constrain one and a
 * body may then act on the fact.
 *
 * Not an open extension point, and that is the contract rather than a limitation: every admitted
 * operation is **always lock-free**, which is only keepable by refusing the widths a target would
 * have to synthesize with a library lock. A library cannot promise that about a type it invents.
 *
 * **`Optional`, and the only rows here that are.** The module has one file, `Atomic.native.yana`,
 * so a JavaScript build reads no declaration of either - which is what `@platform` selection is
 * for and not a library that is missing something. A program on that target cannot write `Atomic`
 * at all, so there is nothing for an unfilled role to break.
 *
 * Zero members: what a class with no members buys is the constraint, and every operation over an
 * atomic is a free function of the module rather than a method of these.
 */
//              role           entity         arity members payload teardown presence
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( atomicValue,   Entity::Class, 1,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( atomicInteger, Entity::Class, 1,    0,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )

/*
 * `Lanewise(v -> m)` - `lib/Core/Vector.yana`, Design-Vector §3.2.
 *
 * What comparing two vectors *is*. A class rather than six functions, and the functional dependency
 * is what earns it: `a .< b` has to infer its own result without an ascription, and one vector shape
 * has exactly one mask shape. `select` is a member rather than a function beside the class because
 * it is the only operation that relates a mask back to the vector it came from, and a class keyed on
 * the pair is where that relation lives.
 *
 * **Seven members, and the order is the dependency.** `expand_provided.cpp` reaches each by
 * position: the six comparisons in the order the language writes them - `.==`, `.!=`, `.<`, `.<=`,
 * `.>`, `.>=` - and `select` last. That order is `lib/Core/Vector.yana`'s and this is the second
 * place it is written down, which is what the column is for.
 *
 * `Optional` on the atomics' terms: the class is declared in Core, so a build that reads a library
 * without it generates nothing rather than reporting.
 */
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( lanewise,      Entity::Class, 2,    7,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )

/*
 * `Bits(a)` - `lib/Core/Bits.yana`, and the counting operations every machine has an instruction
 * for.
 *
 * A class rather than five plain functions for the reason the library's own note gives: R1 admits
 * one plain function per (name, arity) and there are six integer types with an answer here, so a
 * `countBits` per width could never have been six functions - and a user's newtype over a word is a
 * plausible instance, which a class admits and a fixed set of overloads does not.
 *
 * **Five members, and the order is `provided.def`'s dependency**: `countBits`, `leadingZeros`,
 * `trailingZeros`, `bitsUpTo`, `bitWidth`, which is the order `lib/Core/Bits.yana` declares them
 * in. `expand_provided.cpp` reaches each by position, so this column is the second place that order
 * is written down - which is what the column is for.
 *
 * `bitWidth` is the one member that is not an instruction, and it is a member rather than a plain
 * function beside the class because it is the one that has to name the type's own width:
 * `width - leadingZeros(v)`, where a generic body cannot write the width down at all - a
 * subexpression made only of literals resolves on its own and takes `default FromInt`, so
 * `leadingZeros(0)` is an `Int` whatever `a` is.
 *
 * `Optional` on the atomics' and `lanewise`'s terms: the class is the library's, and a build that
 * reads a library without it generates nothing rather than reporting.
 */
YANA_KNOWN_ROLE( bits,          Entity::Class, 1,    5,      0,      false,   Presence::Optional )

/*
 * What joins these when the new stage reads `lib/Core` - Analysis-IR.md §11.4's milestone 8.
 *
 * The classes the language's syntax is written in terms of that are not yet here (`Enum`,
 * `IndexInsert`, `Show`, `Chunked`, and `ByteSwap`, `BitPermute`, `Length` beside them), and the
 * functions it inserts calls to (`checkCondition`, and the three a format expression is built out
 * of).
 *
 * `checkFailed` was on that list and is a row now - see above - and **`checkCondition` stays,
 * because the two are not a pair here.** The shipping stage's check is a *call* to a function whose
 * body holds the branch, for a reason that has nothing to do with checking: a branch emitted inside
 * an expression splits a block a lifted `iter fn` had already recorded. The ownership stage's check
 * is the branch itself, written in `Index.get` and grafted by `@inline(always)`, so there is no
 * condition function for a role to name - and a row for one would be a `Presence` describing a
 * declaration this stage will never reach for.
 *
 * `TrivialCopy`, `TrivialSink` and `Bits` were on that list and are rows now. What moved the first
 * two is that they are `Ownership`'s own classification and `structuralAnswer` could answer them
 * without an instance; what moved `Bits` is that its five members are the machine's instructions,
 * so `provided.def` supplies the instances the way it supplies `Integral`'s.
 *
 * The heap's allocate and free were on that list and are rows now - see `allocateHeap` above. What
 * moved them is that `prelude/Native/Heap.native.yana` declares them and this stage compiles it, so
 * they are no longer roles nothing could fill.
 *
 * They are not here yet because the stage that would bind them cannot compile the parts of `lib/`
 * that declare them. Adding rows for declarations nothing can reach would be a table of roles that
 * are permanently unfilled, which is a worse statement than an absent row: `Presence` would have to
 * lie about every one of them.
 *
 * ## What is still matched by name, and where
 *
 * Written down because "no spelling outside this directory" is the rule this directory exists to
 * enforce.
 *
 * **The classes listed above**, in `core.cpp`'s `definePreludeLookups` and in the instance
 * generators beside it - `classNamed(module, "Show"_v)` and its neighbours. Nothing else: the older
 * resolver binds the class rows here now, so every one of `CoreClasses` that has a row is read out
 * of `Program::knownClasses` and the spelling of each went with the lookup. `TrivialCopy`,
 * `TrivialSink`, `Lanewise` and `Bits` were on the by-name list and came off it the day `prelude/`
 * declared them, which is what that list predicts. What is left - `Enum`, `Index`, `Show`,
 * `Contiguous`, `Chunked`, `Writable`, `IndexInsert`, `ByteSwap`, `BitPermute` and `Length` - is the
 * same paragraph as the one above: a row apiece the day `prelude/` reaches them, and a lie about
 * `Presence` before that.
 *
 * `Proceed` and `Exit` in `resolve_sig.cpp` were the last entry before those and went with the
 * `outcome` row above.
 *
 * ## What used to be here
 *
 * Each of these was a name compared against a literal outside this directory, and each is now one
 * statement in it. The list is kept because it is the argument for the rule:
 *
 *  - `True` and `False` were `primitive.def`'s tail, beside `Bool` itself, and all three are gone:
 *    the `boolean` row above is the declaration they are constructors of, so the compiler holds an
 *    index into a constructor list and no spelling at all. `Int` and `Long` went with them - they
 *    were rows there *and* aliases in the prelude, and `findBuiltin` runs first, so the declaration
 *    was dead.
 *  - The **width of an unadorned literal** was `intType(32, true)` and `floatType(64)` in
 *    `resolveLiteral`. It is `fromInt` and `fromDecimal`'s head defaults now, and the second one had
 *    already diverged: Core's `Float` is `F32`.
 *  - `main` is `role.h`'s `kEntryPointName`. **Both** resolve stages spelled it, so the language
 *    could have renamed its entry point and moved one of them.
 *  - `Core` is `role.h`'s `kCoreModuleName`. Three places spelled it - both resolve stages and the
 *    library locator, which judges a directory by whether it holds `Core/Core.yana`.
 *  - The **fixities** are gone from `resolve_mir/resolve_expr.cpp` entirely and are declared in
 *    `prelude/Core/Core.yana`, which copies `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s block number for number. They had
 *    to move rather than be corrected: the table's numbers and Core's *had already diverged*, so
 *    `a and b shl c` grouped one way under each stage. `doc/spec/declarations.md` is explicit that
 *    "an operator used infix with no fixity declaration in scope is an error - there is no default
 *    precedence", so the fallback the table provided was never the language's anyway.
 *  - **What an operator computes** was `kOperators` and `kPrefixOperators` in the same file - a
 *    spelling per operator beside a `BinaryOp`, standing in for instance selection - and `op.cpp`'s
 *    `operandTypeAllowed` beside it said which types each accepted, standing in for the instances
 *    existing. Both are gone: an operator is a call by name, the classes are the rows above, and the
 *    primitive instances are `provided.def`'s. `Proceed` and `Exit` went in the same change.
 */
